ing  what they would say is sort of a a moral  statement uh you know on the side of  international legality but the real  reason is because uh you know Japan  wants to keep a very close relationship  with the United States uh it's very  concerned about the rise of China and  it's put strong value on the U.S Japan  Alliance so uh in a sense by keeping uh  close with the United States on the  Russia issue it means that Japan uh can  expect a strong backing from the United  States uh when it has its own security  problems possibly uh in East Asia so  that that would be the real key reason  why Japan is going along with this  uh the the area that you talk about in  East Asia forms to be part of the larger  indo-pacific of which Japan is a key  player and a number of Japanese  diplomats have spoken over the past few  days and they have talked about  following the rules-based international  order because it is applicable not just  in the indo-pacific but also in the case  of Ukraine is that also another  rationale behind this  uh it's certainly uh a reason which a  rationale which they will give and it's  uh part of sort of the let's call it the  Diplomatic posture of Japan it is true  that you know the Japanese society and  Japanese within you know their own  country are generally you know rule  abiding people uh but uh you know I  think that if they found their national  interests served uh by breaking the law  so for example let's say on the issue of  whaling uh they will you know they'll do  so uh but in most cases they they they  present themselves as uh uh rule-abiding  uh and and force for the rule of law in  East Asia in the world  journalist Michael Penn in Tokyo thanks  so much for breaking that down thank you
 

